== A. parity codes of the integer cycles on Z ==
  T  (n/2, 3n+1)
    cycle from    1: length  3, word 100, 2-adic numerators mod 7: [3, 5, 6], real numerators: [1, 2, 4]
    cycle from    0: length  1, word 0, 2-adic numerators mod 1: [0], real numerators: [0]
    cycle from   -1: length  2, word 10, 2-adic numerators mod 3: [1, 2], real numerators: [1, 2]
    cycle from   -5: length  5, word 10100, 2-adic numerators mod 31: [11, 13, 21, 22, 26], real numerators: [5, 9, 10, 18, 20]
    cycle from  -17: length 18, word 101010100101010000, 2-adic numerators mod 262143: 18 elements, real numerators: 18 elements
  T1 (n/2,(3n+1)/2)
    cycle from    1: length  2, word 10, 2-adic numerators mod 3: [1, 2], real numerators: [1, 2]
    cycle from    0: length  1, word 0, 2-adic numerators mod 1: [0], real numerators: [0]
    cycle from   -1: length  1, word 1, 2-adic numerators mod 1: [0], real numerators: [0]
    cycle from   -5: length  3, word 110, 2-adic numerators mod 7: [1, 2, 4], real numerators: [3, 5, 6]
    cycle from  -17: length 11, word 11110111000, 2-adic numerators mod 2047: 11 elements, real numerators: 11 elements
PASS A: for n = 1, 4, 2 the first 30 parity bits equal the 2-adic expansion of -c/(2^L-1) = -1/7, -4/7, -2/7

== B. L = 3: Paley QR/NQR and the two sheets ==
  trivial cycle [1, 4, 2] (T), word [1, 0, 0]: real numerators [1, 2, 4], 2-adic numerators [3, 5, 6]
  minus-sheet cycle [-5, -7, -10] (T1), word [1, 1, 0]: real numerators [3, 5, 6], 2-adic numerators [1, 2, 4]
PASS B: trivial cycle: real code = QR_7 = {1,2,4} (Paley connection set), 2-adic code = NQR_7
PASS B: minus-sheet 3-cycle {-5,-7,-10}: real code = NQR_7, 2-adic code = QR_7
PASS B: the complement of the trivial word (011) is the minus-sheet word up to rotation, real code NQR_7
PASS B: under T1 the word 100 is the rational cycle {1/5, 4/5, 2/5} (clock 2^3 - 3 = 5); under T it is {1,4,2} (clock 2^2 - 3 = 1)
PASS B: on {1,4,2} the Collatz map acts as x -> 4x (mod 7) = x/2, a Paley automorphism (4 is a square)

== C. one 2-coset = QR_p for a Mersenne prime p = 2^L - 1 only when L = 3 ==
  L =  2, p = 3: |<2>| = 2, |QR_p| = 1, equal: False
  L =  3, p = 7: |<2>| = 3, |QR_p| = 3, equal: True
  L =  5, p = 31: |<2>| = 5, |QR_p| = 15, equal: False
  L =  7, p = 127: |<2>| = 7, |QR_p| = 63, equal: False
  L = 13, p = 8191: |<2>| = 13, |QR_p| = 4095, equal: False
  L = 17, p = 131071: |<2>| = 17, |QR_p| = 65535, equal: False
  L = 19, p = 524287: |<2>| = 19, |QR_p| = 262143, equal: False
  L = 31, p = 2147483647: |<2>| = 31, |QR_p| = 1073741823, equal: False
PASS C: <2> = QR_p exactly for L = 3 among the Mersenne primes with L <= 31 (equation 2^(L-1) - 1 = L)

== D. Fano / Singer ==
PASS D: {1,2,4} is a (7,3,1) difference set (every nonzero residue once)
  powers of alpha: [1, 2, 4, 3, 6, 7, 5]  exponents with trace 0: [1, 2, 4]
PASS D: {1,2,4} = exponents of alpha^i with trace 0 (x^3+x+1), i.e. the conjugates of alpha; a Fano line
PASS D: the 7 translates of {1,2,4} are the lines of a Fano plane (pairwise meeting in one point)

== E. Aut(Paley_7) and what the Collatz cycle keeps ==
  affine automorphisms: 21; multipliers used: [1, 2, 4]
PASS E: |Aut| = 21 (x -> ax+b, a in QR); the stabiliser of the connection set QR_7 is the multiplier group <x2> of order 3
PASS E: no non-trivial translate of QR_7 is a 2-coset, so none is the code of a cycle (translations have no Collatz counterpart)
PASS E: x -> -x reverses every arc (anti-automorphism), i.e. swaps QR and NQR: the sheet/place swap of B

== F. lonely runner readings ==
  lon({1,2}) = 1/3 at t in ['1/3', '2/3']
PASS F: speeds {1,2} (the T1 trivial cycle) are a tight instance (lon = 1/3 = 1/(k+1)), lonely exactly at t = 1/3, 2/3 = 0.(01)_2, 0.(10)_2, its own real parity code
  lon({1,2,4}) = 1/3 at t in ['1/3', '2/3']; positions at t = 1/7: [1, 2, 4]/7, at t = 3/7: [3, 5, 6]/7
PASS F: speeds {1,2,4} have lon = 1/3 (not tight for k = 3); at t = 1/7 they sit on QR_7/7, at t = 3/7 on NQR_7/7

